WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:07.820
The anterior epithelium, here, which will become a part, here, which is called the cornea, there, you see,

2
00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:14.370
l epithelium, which is therefore a mesenchymal tissue,

3
00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:23.690
whereas the conjunctival tissue, we could say it is an epithelial, thus a limiting tissue.

4
00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:33.640
Then we have what is called the anterior chamber, the pupillary orifice.

5
00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:40.070
You have three major tunics that surround the eye.

6
00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:46.210
So it's as if they were three socks, one inside the other.

7
00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:49.310
The first sock is called the sclera.

8
00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:54.370
It is continuous with the cornea anteriorly.

9
00:00:56.141 --> 00:01:06.901
transparent, then you have the limbus, then the white part. This is the first layer. I remove this first sock, and then, we encounter an important layer called the choroid.

10
00:01:08.721 --> 00:01:14.161
And the choroid, we see here, you see, which is a layer that is extremely vascularized.

11
00:01:15.761 --> 00:01:20.471
And this choroid continues anteriorly, here, with what is called the iris.

12
00:01:24.212 --> 00:01:28.362
Iridology is a reading of the mesodermal vascular system.

13
00:01:31.492 --> 00:01:36.792
By observing the eye, you can see information about trophic changes of a vascular nature.

14
00:01:38.352 --> 00:01:45.462
This is expressed in the iris, which are sensors where they can store, for example, of sulfur. We can store a lot of things.

15
00:01:47.512 --> 00:01:51.672
And we can learn to read. There is a mapping of how it will be arranged.

16
00:01:52.282 --> 00:02:00.282
there will be another relationship that will be important, which is the ciliary processes and then there will be a relationship with what we call the suspensory

17
00:02:00.542 --> 00:02:05.712
muscles of the iris, which we will see in detail later to understand their physiology.

18
00:02:05.842 --> 00:02:11.202
So we see the crystalline lens, the different chambers that will appear, the pupillary orifice,

19
00:02:14.202 --> 00:02:15.952
Here, a little further,

20
00:02:16.402 --> 00:02:19.042
in this choroid with the iris, you see.

21
00:02:20.353 --> 00:02:24.593
which will form an important link with what is called the suspensory ligament.

22
00:02:27.093 --> 00:02:31.343
There is also a ciliary muscle; there is an accommodation system that will occur.

23
00:02:33.173 --> 00:02:42.773
Well, this accommodation system involves this lens, which we call the crystalline lens, by the ciliary muscles, with the ora serrata; well, we'll see that later,

24
00:02:44.273 --> 00:02:51.003
which will increase, change the shape of my lens slightly, to allow the image to integrate correctly on the retinal plane.

25
00:02:56.344 --> 00:02:58.254
First layer, the sclera.

26
00:02:59.884 --> 00:03:01.884
Second layer, the choroid.

27
00:03:02.784 --> 00:03:04.674
Third layer, the retina.

28
00:03:06.744 --> 00:03:11.424
The nervous tissue, the deepest, which is truly the expression of the brain.

29
00:03:11.684 --> 00:03:15.364
With the colobomatous cleft, which gives rise to the anomaly.

30
00:03:16.495 --> 00:03:21.155
for the hyaloid artery and vessels which will form the retinal vessels, etc.

31
00:03:24.695 --> 00:03:27.955
Colobomatous cleft, do you understand what it was?

32
00:03:27.955 --> 00:03:32.165
With, you see, this remnant here, then this closure that will occur.

33
00:03:32.755 --> 00:03:36.835
So the retina, in fact, is the innermost layer of the eye.

34
00:03:37.755 --> 00:03:40.475
And so, where does this retina come from?

35
00:03:42.615 --> 00:03:49.095
Do you remember this first vesicle? this small sphere that emerges, which we call the primary optic vesicle,

36
00:03:50.705 --> 00:03:54.955
which will make a movement like this, you see, like a calyx, a cup.

37
00:03:57.665 --> 00:04:02.615
It is made of two layers, like this, you see, an inner layer, an outer layer.

38
00:04:02.885 --> 00:04:08.955
And this is where you understand what is called a retinal detachment. We can clearly see these two layers.

39
00:04:10.025 --> 00:04:18.915
Well, when people have a retinal detachment, it is this layer that expands, it is this space between the inner leaflet and the outer leaflet of the retina.

40
00:04:20.156 --> 00:04:26.656
There will be another very thin layer here, which will be very important later, it is what is called the pigmentary layer.

41
00:04:28.716 --> 00:04:39.276
You will discuss this, I think, in detail tomorrow, it is the entire layer of this retina with the pigmentary layer, and then what are called the photoreceptors, which are of the type that you...

42
00:04:40.707 --> 00:04:47.987
know as cones and rods. But you also have a layer called the ganglion cells. It is truly an important layer for

43
00:04:52.187 --> 00:04:58.667
information transfer to enable transduction. It is the conversion of a photon into electrical energy.

44
00:04:58.747 --> 00:05:00.747
Change members to sea.

